course: everyday
lesson: 376
title: What fights cost at home
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: When Jake was suspended for fighting, the school phoned his dad at the warehouse. Dad had to leave his shift halfway, lost half a day's pay, and drove Jake home without saying a word. At home Jake's little brother Alfie took one look at the new bruise on Jake's cheek and burst into tears, the way he did every time. Jake had thought the fight was over when the bell rang. It wasn't — it had come home with him.
explain: Maddie's street scrap ended with a stone through Mr Patel's window, and her mum paid for the glass out of the holiday money. Then came the wrist: Maddie fell in a fight by the garages, her dad took her to A&E, and while they waited four hours for the X-ray he missed the job interview he had ironed his shirt for. He told her it didn't matter. It did, a bit, and they both knew it.
explain: Fights cost more than bruises. They cost money, time off work, trust and a family's good name. At the school fete other parents quietly moved Jake's family to another table, and another mum told Maddie's mum she was «raising a little thug». Neither mum had thrown a single punch. Jake's mum stopped trusting him to walk home alone for a while. And after Kieran's scrap by the chippy, a police officer came round to his house; his mum went white.
explain: Even quiet Sam's family paid: his mum took a day off work to go to a meeting about the bus-shelter boys who picked on him. The person who fights is rarely the only one who pays.
fact: When a child is suspended, a parent often has to leave work to collect them and look after them at home.
fact: Missing work can mean losing pay, and some jobs or interviews do not come back.
fact: Damage done in a fight, like a broken window, usually has to be paid for by the family.
fact: After a serious street fight the police may visit the family at home.
fact: Parents who see a child fighting often stop trusting them to go out alone for a while.
fact: Younger brothers and sisters are often frightened and upset when they see an older child hurt.
fact: Other parents may judge a whole family by one child's fights, even though it is unfair.
fact: A broken bone means hours in hospital, weeks in a cast and no sport for a long time.
fact: Even the child who is picked on costs the family time, because parents must go to meetings to stop it.
fact: Trust lost through fighting comes back slowly, by keeping out of trouble day after day.
exercise: What did Jake's dad lose when he had to collect Jake after the suspension? | Half a day's pay from his warehouse shift | Nothing, because schools pay parents | Only his lunch break, which he spent in the car park | His driving licence
exercise: How did Jake's little brother react to Jake's new bruise? | He burst into tears, as he did every time | He laughed at the black eye and asked who wanted a fight | He didn't notice it | He told Jake it looked cool
exercise: Why did Jake think the fight was over when the bell rang? | He thought fights end at school and don't follow you home | The teacher told him it was over | His dad had told him so on the phone that morning | The other boy said sorry
exercise: Who paid for Mr Patel's broken window? | Maddie's mum, out of the holiday money | Mr Patel himself, because it was his window | The school, out of the money set aside for trips | The police, because Mr Patel phoned them
exercise: What did Maddie's dad miss while waiting at A&E? | A job interview he had got ready for | A football match on TV that he had been looking forward to | Nothing, because the wait was very short | His own doctor's appointment
exercise: How do you think Maddie felt when her dad said the interview didn't matter? | Guilty, because she knew it did matter a bit | Proud, because he wasn't cross | Happy, because she got a whole day off school | Nothing, because it wasn't her fault at all
exercise: Why did a police officer visit Kieran's family? | Because Kieran was in a street fight | Because they had won a prize in a raffle | Because the officer was a neighbour who popped round for tea | Because Kieran had lost his new bike again
exercise: What did Jake's mum stop doing after his fights? | Trusting him to walk home alone | Making his packed lunch every morning | Talking to his teacher every day | Buying him new shoes for walking to school
exercise: Why did Sam's mum take a day off work? | To go to a meeting about the boys who picked on Sam | Because Sam had started a fight | To take Sam to the cinema for a treat after school | Because the school was closed
exercise: At the fete, why were Jake's family moved to another table? | Other parents didn't want to sit near the family of a boy who fights | There were too many chairs at that table already | Jake's dad asked to move | Their table was too close to the cakes
exercise: Is it fair that another mum called Maddie's mum's child «a little thug»? | No, but people often judge a whole family by one child | Yes, mums are to blame for every fight their children have | Yes, because her mum taught her to fight | No, because Maddie has never fought
exercise: What is the wise way for Jake to win back his mum's trust? | Keep out of fights day after day until she sees the change | Promise her once and then forget all about it after a week | Win every fight so she stops worrying | Hide his bruises under a hoodie
exercise: Which costs of a fight can't be fixed with money? | A little brother's fear and a family's lost trust | A broken window in the kitchen | A cinema ticket | A cinema ticket and some sweets that he'd saved up for
exercise: Who usually pays for a fight? | The fighter and many people around them | Only the person who loses | Only the person who wins | Nobody at all, as long as no teacher ever sees it
exercise: What kind of courage could Jake show at home instead of hiding? | Telling his dad he's sorry for the lost pay | Starting a bigger fight to look tough | Pretending the bruise came from football | Blaming his little brother
exercise: Why might a stranger at the fete be unfair to Jake's mum? | She didn't throw a punch but got judged for one | She had started the fight herself in the playground | She was rude to the stranger first | She brought no cake to the fete
test: «my dad had to leave work cos i got sent home lol». Is it actually funny? | No, he probably lost pay and got in trouble at work | Yes, he gets a free afternoon | Yes, dads love any excuse to leave work early | No, because schools never call parents
test: Kid at the rec: «just smash his window, his mum'll pay, not our problem». What's true? | Damage costs a family real money and it's wrong | His mum will happily pay for it out of her purse | Windows are free to replace | Nobody ever pays for broken windows, they just get fixed
test: «my little bro cries every time i come home with a black eye, he's such a baby». What's going on? | He's scared because he loves his big brother | He's just being silly | He wants the black eye himself | He's pretending to be scared so he gets more attention from Mum
test: «a copper came round ours after the scrap by the chippy, mum went white». Why was mum so upset? | The fight had brought the police to the family's door | She didn't like the officer's hat or the way he talked to her | She thought it was a party | She'd forgotten to make tea
test: Mate: «ur mum won't let u walk home alone?? lmao baby». What's the most likely reason? | She stopped trusting him after his fights | She wants to buy him sweets | She thinks walking alone is bad for his legs and his knees | She forgot the way to school
test: «Maddie's dad missed his job thing cos she bust her wrist fighting, gutted for him». Which is true? | One fight can cost a parent a big chance | He can always get the same interview next day | Fights only hurt the fighter | A wrist heals in a day
test: Teacher: «Who pays when you fight, Jake?» Best honest answer? | Me, my family and the other kid's family too | Only the other kid | Nobody at all, because it's all sorted now with the head | Just the school
test: «some posh mum said maddie's mum is raising a thug. well rude». What's fair to say? | It's unkind to blame the mum, but fights do give a family a bad name | The posh mum is totally right | Maddie's mum taught her to fight | It doesn't matter at all what other people say about a family, ever
test: «my mum took a day off for that meeting about the bus shelter lads, cost her loads». Who was really at fault? | The boys who picked on Sam | Sam, for being picked on so much | Sam's mum, for going to the school | The bus driver, for not stopping the boys on the bus
test: «just hit him back, what's the worst that can happen». Which is the most honest answer? | Suspension, injuries, police and a family that pays for it | Nothing, if you hit hard enough | Only a telling-off | You get famous at school and nobody picks on you again
test: «jake says his dad's not cross, just quiet». What does the quiet probably mean? | Dad is upset and worried but holding it in | Dad is happy about the fight and proud of his son | Dad didn't hear anything about the fight yet | Dad is just tired after playing football all day
exam: Identify the principal category of persons who incur costs consequent upon a pupil's participation in a fight. | The pupil's family and others, in addition to the pupil | Exclusively the pupil adjudged to have lost | Exclusively the school authorities | No person whatsoever, provided that no injury of any kind is sustained by anyone
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the financial liability arising from property damaged in the course of a street altercation? | It ordinarily falls upon the family of the child responsible | It is invariably met by the local council | It is entirely extinguished in any case where the damage was unintentional | It is borne by the owner of the property
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning parental trust subsequent to repeated fighting. | It is restored gradually through sustained good conduct | It is restored immediately and completely upon a single sincere promise | It is never diminished by a child's conduct | It is restored solely by the payment of money
exam: Ascertain the most probable reason for which the younger sibling of a habitual fighter becomes distressed upon the latter's return home. | Fear and concern occasioned by visible injuries to a loved one | Envy of the injuries sustained by the elder sibling in the course of the fight | Indifference to the sibling's welfare | Displeasure at a delayed mealtime
